					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-12938119#post-4471646</guid>
				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-12938119/scp-1964-ex#post-4471646</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Fri, 17 Jan 2020 18:22:14 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>aismallard</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>4598089</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>I'm a fan of -EX skips, but this didn't do it for me.</p> <p>Exploring the strangeness around particle physics is certainly a good place for exploring the strangeness of the normal world, but I wasn't a fan of the execution here. Asking random anomalies for information about quantum properties doesn't make much sense, and the SCP-three blackbox that was used for crosstesting isn't hinted enough to make much sense here. Why this anomaly in particular?</p> <p>Furthermore, one of the critical parts in an explained article (at least in my view), is <em>how</em> the Foundation discovers it was non-anomalous. Here it's primarily &quot;oh we found out it's regular science&quot;, without much of an justification.</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
